 "capsule": "'Phoenician Empire' is a loose modern label for the wider Mediterranean network of Phoenician colonies and trading posts — including Carthage, Gadir (Cádiz), Utica, and outposts in Cyprus, Sardinia, and Sicily — rather than for a single centralized state ruled from the Levantine homeland. Unlike Phoenicia's autonomous city-kingdoms, this colonial web had no unified sovereign; its most powerful node, Carthage, only emerged as a genuine imperial power over the western Mediterranean centuries after its traditional founding date, eventually overshadowing older colonies like Utica and Gadir. This loosely termed 'empire' effectively ended with Carthage's destruction by Rome in 146 BCE.",
